# XSH nice()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# nice() is XSI. It changes the nice value of the whole process and cannot
# be undone without privilege when it raises the value, so every test that
# calls it works in a child process. Scheduling effects are never observed:
# only the return values, errno and the nice value read back with
# getpriority() (which returns the nice value minus NZERO, as nice() does).
# getpriority() is a separate XSI page but is used here as the page's own
# "see also". The value reached when the request would pass the upper
# limit is taken as the highest value the implementation enforces: the
# nice page says 2*NZERO-1 but the getpriority page lets an implementation
# enforce more restrictive limits (see the report), so the tests only
# require a value at or below NZERO-1 that does not change on repeating the
# request. Privilege is not assumed in either direction (EPERM test).

assertion  XSH.nice.001
anchor     functions/nice.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nice.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       nice(0) returns the current nice value minus NZERO: a value from
           -NZERO to NZERO-1, equal to getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, 0), and the
           same again when repeated.
test       t/001-nice-zero.c

assertion  XSH.nice.002
anchor     functions/nice.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nice.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       nice(1) adds 1 to the nice value: it returns the value from
           nice(0) plus 1 and getpriority() then reports that value; the
           only other outcome allowed is the old value returned unchanged
           when that is the highest value the implementation enforces
           (confirmed by a larger request returning the same value).
note       Q-86: the upper limit may be lower than NZERO-1.
test       t/002-nice-adds.c

assertion  XSH.nice.003
anchor     functions/nice.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A request past the upper limit sets the nice value to the limit
           and is not an error: nice(2*NZERO+5) returns a value from the old
           value up to NZERO-1, getpriority() reports the same value, and
           repeating the request returns it again.
note       Q-86: the upper limit may be lower than NZERO-1.
test       t/003-nice-clamps.c

assertion  XSH.nice.004
anchor     functions/nice.html#ERRORS/EPERM
anchor     functions/nice.html#RETURN VALUE
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       A process without appropriate privileges that calls nice(-1)
           gets -1 with EPERM, and its nice value is not changed. The test
           is UNRESOLVED when the call succeeds (the process may have
           appropriate privileges).
note       If the effective user ID is 0 the child first tries to give up
           privilege with setgid() and setuid() to an unlikely user ID.
test       t/004-nice-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.nice.005
anchor     functions/nice.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      2001
says       The nice value set applies to the process, not to the calling
           thread: after one thread calls nice(1), getpriority(PRIO_PROCESS,
           0) called from another thread of the process reports the new
           value.
test       t/005-nice-process-wide.c
